    <id>https://doi.org/10.1093/restud/rdag103</id>
    <title>Demographics, Wealth, and Global Imbalances in the Twenty-First Century</title>
    <updated>2026-09-16T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Adrien Auclert, Hannes Malmberg, Frédéric Martenet, Matthew Rognlie</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Review of Economic Studies&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;We use a sufficient statistic approach to quantify the general equilibrium effects of population ageing on the returns to wealth, wealth accumulation, and global imbalances. Combining population forecasts with household survey data from twenty-five countries, we measure the compositional effect of ageing: how a changing age distribution affects wealth-to-GDP, holding the age profiles of wealth and labour income fixed. In a baseline overlapping generations model this statistic, in conjunction with cross-sectional information and two standard macro parameters, pins down general equilibrium outcomes. Since the compositional effect is positive, large, and heterogeneous across countries, our model predicts that population ageing will lower returns to wealth, increase wealth-to-GDP ratios, and widen global imbalances through the twenty-first century. These conclusions extend to a richer model in which bequests, individual savings, and the tax-and-transfer system all respond to demographic change.</content>
