    <id>https://doi.org/10.1002/smj.70132</id>
    <title>Designing for unanticipated uses: How product attributes shape community‐led exaptation</title>
    <updated>2026-09-27T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Shi‐Ying Lim, Tian Heong Chan</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Strategic Management Journal&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Users often discover new uses for products that firms may later commercialize, which we call community‐led exaptation. We examine how product attributes shape users' discovery of new functions versus new functionalities. Using 1925 IKEA product hacks, we find that modularity increases the likelihood of new functions. This effect strengthens when product components are compatible across product lines but weakens when esthetic product variants exist. Visual simplicity, conversely, increases the likelihood of new functionalities. These findings suggest that firms can structure the opportunity space for discovery through product design. Exploratory analysis of archival data and interviews further illustrates how IKEA responds to these discoveries. We contribute to exaptation, user innovation, and design research by showing how product attributes shape the types of new uses communities discover.Managerial SummaryHow can firms design products to facilitate community‐led exaptation? We find that product attributes can shape what users discover. Modular products enable users to find new functions (i.e., applications), while visually simple products enable users to find new functionalities (i.e., new product capabilities). Firms' decisions to offer components that are compatible across product lines and esthetic product variants can influence the likelihood of new functions. Additionally, firms can capitalize on these new uses by showcasing promising community‐discovered functions on official channels or incorporate them as complements to existing product lines. However, functional modifications can raise safety concerns and may require additional development. Firms should therefore design for intended use, and for the kinds of new uses they are willing to seed and commercialize.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1002/smj.70132"/>
    <category term="Strategic Management Journal"/>
    <published>2026-09-27T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1002/hrm.70102</id>
    <title>From Individual Advancement to Structural Conditions: Reframing Leadership Development</title>
    <updated>2026-09-27T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Michele Heath, Jamila Maxie, Anwesha Choudhury</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Human Resource Management&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Low‐wage workers represent a significant segment of the organizational workforce, yet they remain largely absent from leadership development research and practice. These workers routinely face barriers such as limited access to development opportunities and occupational stigma. However, leadership development research has primarily emphasized individual readiness and human capital, paying too little attention to how leadership systems shape access to development. Drawing on Scarcity Theory and Pierre Bourdieu's Theory, we argue that leadership development operates as a classed system influenced by both material constraints and cultural expectations. These dynamics disadvantage low‐wage workers whose everyday leadership contributions often go unrecognized. We contribute to human resource management scholarship by integrating social class and scarcity into leadership development research by showing how economic precarity and class‐based norms jointly shape limited access to leadership development for the low‐wage worker. The proposed conceptual model reframes leadership development as a more inclusive talent management practice capable of leveraging a broader range of organizational expertise. We conclude by outlining future research directions on how HR systems can reduce structural barriers and expand leadership pathways for low‐wage workers.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1002/hrm.70102"/>
    <category term="Human Resource Management"/>
    <published>2026-09-27T00:00:00+00:00</published>
